<section>
  <p class="kicker"><%= link_to_page "ads-l", label_type: :nav, class: "cta" %></p>
  <h1>The declaration skyBlip Go needs, and the documents that go with it.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    ADS-L has no ETSO and no MOPS. Under <strong>ADS-L.4.SRD860.A.4</strong>
    the manufacturer verifies its equipment against the specification and
    declares conformity on the template in the specification&rsquo;s appendix.
    Under <strong>A.5</strong> it ships four documents with the product: that
    declaration, the declaration of conformity behind the CE marking, an
    installation manual and a user manual.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page holds the declaration for skyBlip Go, filled in as far as the
    facts allow, and links each of the four documents. It will be signed in
    November or December 2026. The four statements the manufacturer must make
    hold, and the last table below gives the grounds for each. The firmware
    now transmits the configured ICAO address under F.2.3. Our reading that
    this overrides the manufacturer&rsquo;s address on every payload remains
    subject to confirmation by EASA, as recorded on the
    <%= link_to_page "ads-l/questions", label: "open questions page" %>.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Firmware and hardware</h2>
  <h3>skyBlip supplies the firmware, declared on one hardware: the LilyGO T-Echo.</h3>

  <p>
    skyBlip Go is skyBlip&rsquo;s firmware on a LilyGO T-Echo. LilyGO builds
    the hardware, and the T-Echo has passed CE testing. The declaration
    covers the firmware on the T-Echo. skyBlip will revise it before it
    supports any other hardware.
  </p>
  <p>
    The firmware checks the hardware it runs on. At every start-up it probes
    the SX1262 radio by a register written and read back, and the BME280
    barometer by its chip id. Without either, the unit does not run: it shows
    its self-test page, neither transmits nor receives, and cannot be
    activated, because no service runs to answer the activation. A part that
    fails or is removed after activation stops the unit at its next
    start-up. A compile-time check rejects a build whose programmed power,
    with the declared antenna, would exceed 14&nbsp;dBm e.r.p.
  </p>
</section>

