<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The grounds</h2>
  <h3>What stands behind each field and statement.</h3>

  <p>The declaration names skyBlip firmware v1, which carries skyBlip&rsquo;s ADS-L protocol v1, for ADS-L 4 SRD-860 Issue 2. Every v1 release of the protocol is covered, from v1.0.0. Development builds carry 0.1.0 until the first production release. The ADS-L protocol version is separate from the packet&rsquo;s Protocol Version field in E.1.1. From v1.0.0, it changes only when ADS-L behaviour changes. Each firmware release lists the protocol versions it carries. A unit reports its ADS-L version through the companion link&rsquo;s <code>protocols</code> command, and the briefing app&rsquo;s device page displays it.</p>

  <h3>Transmission parameters</h3>
  <p>M-band only. Traffic uses 868.2&nbsp;MHz in an even UTC second and 868.4&nbsp;MHz in an odd one. Status uses the other channel. Traffic and Status bursts start and finish inside C.5&rsquo;s direct slot, 450 to 1000&nbsp;ms after the UTC second. Their instants are drawn from the unit&rsquo;s own address and UTC second, not from the configured ICAO address. The SX1262 is programmed for nominal +14&nbsp;dBm conducted power, with a datasheet tolerance of &plusmn;2&nbsp;dB. A 0.5&nbsp;dB feed loss allowance and the antenna&rsquo;s datasheet peak gain of 1.6&nbsp;dBi give a computed e.r.p. of 12.95&nbsp;dBm after the 2.15&nbsp;dB conversion from dBi to dBd. A compile-time check rejects a computed e.r.p. above 14&nbsp;dBm, the M-band limit in C.2. Output power, antenna gain and feed loss have not been measured on a unit. The appendix asks for 12 to 14&nbsp;dBm e.r.p. on M-band. Nothing is transmitted on the O-band. The declaration states the 14&nbsp;dBm the firmware sets.</p>
  <p>No optional parameters within the defined Traffic and Status payloads. Both payloads are required by the appendix declaration. No secure signature (E.4) is appended. Each packet carries the lowest Protocol Version whose payload encoding is the same, as E.1.1 requires. Status always carries Protocol Version 1.</p>

  <p>Channel access uses EN 300 220-2 band M&rsquo;s 1&nbsp;% duty-cycle limit over any hour. The firmware refuses a burst past that budget. D.3 also requires listen-before-talk. Before an unforced burst, Go assesses its channel for 200&nbsp;&micro;s, ending at most 2&nbsp;ms before transmission. The -78.99&nbsp;dBm limit for a 0&nbsp;dBd antenna becomes -78.44&nbsp;dBm at the antenna connector after correction for the antenna&rsquo;s datasheet gain of -0.55&nbsp;dBd. The 0.5&nbsp;dB feed loss allowance between connector and chip puts the chip&rsquo;s busy threshold at -78.94&nbsp;dBm. Neither gain nor loss has been measured on a unit. The SX1262&rsquo;s RSSI is uncalibrated, with no margin for its error. This is a known limit of the declaration. A busy channel defers the burst by a random 3 to 50 units of 5&nbsp;ms, C.2&rsquo;s backoff interval of 15 to 250&nbsp;ms, as far as its window reaches. Only on the ground may Go force it after 3000&nbsp;ms, followed by 2000&nbsp;ms without its own transmissions.</p>

  <p>The Status payload, type 3, is scheduled twice per 20&nbsp;s cycle, airborne and on the ground in the same seconds. The cycle starts at the even second <code>c = 2 * (serial mod 10)</code>, where the serial is the low 16 bits of the unit&rsquo;s own address, not the configured ICAO address. Status goes at c+2 on 868.4&nbsp;MHz and c+11 on 868.2&nbsp;MHz. The intervals are 9 then 11&nbsp;s. Each channel carries one Status every 20&nbsp;s, meeting G.2.11&rsquo;s minimum for a receiver listening on one channel. These are nominal rates. An unsent burst retries at 2&nbsp;s intervals on the same channel when its dwell is free, until sent or its type&rsquo;s next turn is due.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Status field</th><th>Declared value</th><th>Clause</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Max. Protocol Version</th><td>1</td><td>G.2.1</td></tr>
        <tr><th>M-band receive</th><td>2, Partial</td><td>G.2.2</td></tr>
        <tr><th>O-band LDR receive</th><td>0</td><td>G.2.3</td></tr>
        <tr><th>O-band HDR receive</th><td>0. Reception is confined to the uplink slot, which this field excludes</td><td>G.2.4</td></tr>
        <tr><th>ADS-L 4 Mobile</th><td>0</td><td>G.2.5</td></tr>
        <tr><th>E-conspicuity receive</th><td>Bit 0, FLARM-generation. Bit 4, ADS-B 1090, is also set while a paired Bravo is live</td><td>G.2.6</td></tr>
        <tr><th>E-conspicuity transmit</th><td>Bit 4, ADS-B 1090, once the aircraft&rsquo;s transponder is known to send it. 0 otherwise, which the clause reads as unknown</td><td>G.2.7</td></tr>
        <tr><th>XPDR</th><td>2, Mode S, once the aircraft&rsquo;s transponder is known. 0 otherwise, which the clause reads as unknown</td><td>G.2.8</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Traffic Uplink client</th><td>1</td><td>G.2.9</td></tr>
        <tr><th>FIS-B client</th><td>0</td><td>G.2.10</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The aircraft&rsquo;s transponder is known once the pilot has entered the aircraft&rsquo;s ICAO address and a paired Bravo, on the ground, hears the transponder at that address. A transponder heard at a 24-bit address is Mode S. Once Bravo hears it identify in an extended squitter, it also sends ADS-B 1090. Neither source is enough alone: aircraft without a transponder can have an ICAO address, and the loudest transponder on an apron can be a neighbour&rsquo;s. Go keeps what it confirmed for as long as the entered address stays the same and the unit runs, so the Status still declares it in flight. Two limits follow from that. A transponder switched off or failed in flight stays declared until the address changes or the unit restarts, since Bravo names it on the ground only. A transponder that sends ADS-B positions but no identification is not counted as ADS-B out, so the Status can declare less than the aircraft has, never more.</p>
  <p class="note">The callsign record is scheduled in slot 1&rsquo;s tail in seconds c+4 and c+14, from 1000 to 1190&nbsp;ms after each second starts, on 868.4&nbsp;MHz. Those seconds follow the scheduled ground positions by 4 and 5&nbsp;s respectively. The tail falls in the next UTC second&rsquo;s reserved interval under C.5. skyBlip treats the record as its own payload, but it travels in the same ADS-L frame, with the same sync word, scrambling and CRC-24. Transmitting there is a deliberate deviation from C.5. The <%= link_to_page "ads-l/callsign", label: "callsign page" %> explains the record and its planned replacement by Issue 3.</p>

  <h3>What the manufacturer additionally declares</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Statement</th><th>Holds</th><th>Why</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>Design processes and production controls</th>
          <td>yes</td>
          <td>LilyGO manufactures the T-Echo under its own production controls, and the model passed BACL Shenzhen&rsquo;s CE tests. skyBlip designs the firmware, and CI runs the ADS-L component and device conformance suites, the EN 300 220 suite and both clause checks on every pull request and push to main that changes the firmware. The ADS-L suites have at least one case for each of Issue 2&rsquo;s 80 clause headings. Nineteen cases are skipped: nine prose, two paperwork and eight optional. None is skipped as not implemented. The cases for F.2.3, the last clause marked as not implemented, now pass. Go refuses <code>FFFFFF</code>, an address no aircraft holds, and, on the ground while a paired live Bravo names the aircraft&rsquo;s transponder, an entered address that differs from it. A clear is never refused. Each unit is checked again where it is used: at every start-up the firmware refuses to run without its radio and barometer, and skyblip.eu activates each unit on its own, signing its serial against its chip id. These software checks do not replace measurements on a unit.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Hardware has passed CE</th>
          <td>yes</td>
          <td>the T-Echo passed BACL Shenzhen&rsquo;s tests for radio, EMC, safety and health under the Radio Equipment Directive. The declaration states this in place of the template&rsquo;s wording, which says the product is CE marked.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Approved address range, F.2.2</th>
          <td>yes</td>
          <td>AMT entry 58, manufacturer page 49, prefix 0, assigned by the registry. The unit retains that assignment while transmitting under AMT entry 5 with the aircraft&rsquo;s configured ICAO address under F.2.3.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Traffic and Status payloads, Subpart G</th>
          <td>yes</td>
          <td>Traffic is scheduled at 1&nbsp;Hz airborne and 0.1&nbsp;Hz on average on the ground, with ground positions 9 then 11&nbsp;s apart. This is skyBlip&rsquo;s reading of G.1.16 with C.2.5&rsquo;s channel alternation. Status, payload type 3, is scheduled twice per 20&nbsp;s cycle, 9 then 11&nbsp;s apart. Each channel carries one Status every 20&nbsp;s, meeting G.2.11&rsquo;s minimum. Under B.5, Traffic uses Issue 1 encoding beside a close neighbour that reads only Issue 1. A position with unknown altitude, ground speed or vertical rate is held rather than sent with a false Issue 1 value.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

